WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.980
Exhumed metamorphic rocks preserve minerals and structures formed at depth.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.440
Laboratory experiments test how rock responds to pressure and temperature.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:14.140
Numerical models explore possible flow and wave paths under stated physical

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.940
assumptions. Models are tested with observations;

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:19.940
they are not direct photographs of inaccessible depths.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:24.520
Together these approaches constrain conditions and processes that a surface exposure

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:25.940
alone cannot reveal.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.400
Seismic tomography combines many rays passing through a region from different

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.540
directions. Earlier-than-expected arrivals can indicate faster material;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.320
later arrivals can indicate slower material.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:40.560
In the supplied diagram, the granite body is a faster region

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.700
relative to surrounding sedimentary rocks.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.940
Rays that sample it can arrive earlier than predicted by a

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:50.420
uniform-background model. The red and blue colors encode relative velocity in

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:53.800
this particular figure. They are not literal rock colors, and they

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:55.580
are not the lab’s reading-emphasis colors.

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:57.460
Sparse ray coverage limits resolution.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:01.180
Slower regions can be consistent with hotter material;

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:05.240
faster regions can reflect cooler or stiffer material.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:09.960
Composition and other physical properties also matter, so velocity alone is

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.900
not a unique thermometer. The chapter presents models with cool subducted

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:17.500
slabs sinking and hot, mostly solid material rising.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.900
It discusses the D-double-prime region near the core–mantle boundary and possible

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.800
links to plumes. The mantle can flow over geological time while

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:30.380
remaining mostly solid. The source treats some deep-flow interpretations as debated

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:33.620
and does not place every mid-ocean ridge above a deep plume.
